WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.480
David, we often talk about the bloated U.S.
budget.

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:08.040
What would you cut from the U.S. budget if
given a choice?

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:14.270
So this is a difficult conversation to have
because the reality about the budget is that

00:00:14.270 --> 00:00:16.880
everything is a negotiation.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:19.539
Not all cuts are created equal.

00:00:19.539 --> 00:00:20.830
Not all cuts save money.

00:00:20.830 --> 00:00:25.869
Sometimes you actually have to spend money
in order to save money by increasing efficiency.

00:00:25.869 --> 00:00:28.800
So I think that that's important to keep in
mind.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:35.500
It's also really important to look at agencies
and see which agencies are actually doing

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:37.610
what they were put in place to do.

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:39.140
Where is their efficiency?

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:41.270
Where is their inefficiency?

00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:45.720
What purposes do different agencies and departments
serve?

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:49.190
So this is not coming from some, you know,
small government libertarian idea of let's

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:53.450
start cutting departments because we just
want to make government small.

00:00:53.450 --> 00:00:57.610
This is the reality that there's probably
a lot of consolidation and things that can

00:00:57.610 --> 00:00:58.610
be moved around.

00:00:58.610 --> 00:01:01.360
But just like to take the lowest hanging fruit
first.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:06.120
The military and Department of Defense obviously
are bloated and need to be cut.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:09.909
We don't need 50 bases in Germany to the extent
Trump wants to reduce that.

00:01:09.909 --> 00:01:11.560
I agree with that.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:14.340
The way in which it's done, obviously, is
the question.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:17.689
We have a massive amount.

00:01:17.689 --> 00:01:20.289
The thing is, it's just so easy to say this.

00:01:20.289 --> 00:01:21.979
Say, OK, I'm on the left.

00:01:21.979 --> 00:01:22.979
We should cut.

00:01:22.979 --> 00:01:24.579
The military will still be safe.

00:01:24.579 --> 00:01:30.899
I think it's more interesting to look, for
example, at cutting specifically military

00:01:30.899 --> 00:01:32.969
contractor spending.

00:01:32.969 --> 00:01:39.719
That's a much more specific angle where the
right and many of the on the left in in Congress

00:01:39.719 --> 00:01:47.249
just are absolutely tied to these groups that
lobby for more military contractor spending

00:01:47.249 --> 00:01:50.100
that specifically needs to be reduced.

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:56.590
You can probably consolidate, for example,
you know, does TSA, Customs and Border.

00:01:56.590 --> 00:02:00.020
Patrol people want ice abolished altogether.

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:08.759
There's probably a big opportunity there to
like, dissolve, reformulate, consolidate.

00:02:08.759 --> 00:02:12.000
That would represent a cost savings.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:15.370
And then there's a bunch of spend money to
save money sort of considerations.

00:02:15.370 --> 00:02:20.840
There is a lot of stuff where we need more
money in the short term, like infrastructure,

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:26.379
upgrading buildings, switching federal buildings
to renewable energy, et cetera, et cetera.

00:02:26.379 --> 00:02:27.769
We're in the short term.

00:02:27.769 --> 00:02:30.099
We actually have to spend more over the long
term.

00:02:30.099 --> 00:02:31.629
We will save.

00:02:31.629 --> 00:02:38.000
The problem with these conversations about
what to cut is that once you understand, you

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:39.000
can't do.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.269
I want to say this.

00:02:41.269 --> 00:02:43.050
Everything's a negotiation.

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:45.140
There's tons of favors and amendments.

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:48.390
And this is sort of like how policy is done
right now.

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:54.050
And to really cut properly, to have a shot
at actually cutting the types of spending

00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:59.829
that is the problematic spending, you need
to completely change so many aspects of our

00:02:59.829 --> 00:03:01.420
political system.

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:07.150
And there's a lot of good books written about
this and articles about it.

00:03:07.150 --> 00:03:12.010
But, you know, me listing stuff maybe is interesting
and I don't think I'd have anything super

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:13.010
surprising.

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:18.860
But what we need to actually change is the
way that this system of cronyism and, oh,

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:23.269
you know, wink, wink, I'll go for your amendment
if you go for mine and this poor, you know,

00:03:23.269 --> 00:03:27.409
everybody's put the truth is we talk about
pork barrel projects.

00:03:27.409 --> 00:03:32.329
Everybody's pork, you know, everybody's pork
is someone else's.

00:03:32.329 --> 00:03:34.420
In many cases, community projects.

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:35.420
Right.

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:42.300
So, you know, I get you to sign off on rebuilding
some ice rinks in my district.

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:48.140
And that seems like pork if you're representing
Hawai and I'm in Illinois, but then you reverse

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:49.140
it.

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:51.950
And we have very similar situations looking
from Illinois over to Hawaii.

00:03:51.950 --> 00:03:55.439
And this is really part of the problem and
part of the problem of how budgeting is done.

00:03:55.439 --> 00:04:01.439
But all of that being said, Department of
Defense, military, absolutely huge opportunities

00:04:01.439 --> 00:04:02.969
to cut for sure.

00:04:02.969 --> 00:04:04.490
No question about it.

00:04:04.490 --> 00:04:05.490
